My responses in {color:#ff991f}*orange.* {color}

* In response to incidents on the Platform where drivers don’t have authorisation to use vehicles on our Platform: *how does this happen?* {color:#ff991f}*_Most drivers rent their vehicles through informal arrangements with the vehicle owner. Sometimes the owner does not know that the driver is using the vehicle on our Platform or they do know. and may at any point ask the driver to withdraw the vehicle from the Platform- which we would obviously not know about until an incident happens that requires contact with the owner e.g accident involving the vehicle._* {color}
* *When Drivers sign up with a vehicle that doesn’t belong to them, do we require proof of authorisation to use that vehicle in other countries*? {color:#ff991f}*No, we don’t.* {color}
* *assuming this happens during Driver sign-up*? {color:#ff991f}*As we don’t require authorisation, not relevant but if we did- it would happen during sign up* {color}

Insofar as Bolt processes personal data of a third party, it will need a lawful basis to do so. If the owner of the vehicle will sign a consent form, that would be ideal. We would need to link a Privacy Notice for their data (if we have one?). {color:#ff991f}*Do we need a privacy notice other than the one applicable to drivers?* {color}

# From a privacy-by-design point of view, and to comply with minimality requirements, it would be important to only process information that is +necessary+ to achieve the intended purpose (confirming permission to operate a third-parties vehicle). {color:#ff991f}*Yes, that would be the case- confirming ownership and confirming that owner has given consent for the driver to use the car on the Bolt Platform.* {color}

