*-* 🇱🇧 *Lebanon* 
{color:#ff991f}+*Registration*+{color}
Lebanon is a light weight market and currently it is not possible for me to determine how extensive the business activity is. In connection with Art 95 Law No. 81 Relating to Electronic Transactions and Personal Data, which requires information to be provided to the authorities prior to the collection of personal data, it cannot be determined beyond doubt whether prior information is required for foreign controllers: 

*1. Territorial Scope:* The data protection provisions shall apply to all automatic and non-automatic processing of data of a personal nature. *The Law does not explicitly provide for the extraterritorial application of any data protection provisions.*

*2. Exemptions:* Art. 94 states that - among others - no permit or license shall be required to process personal data in the following cases:
_- In the case of processes the subject thereof is the_ *_employees or members_* _of the institutions, commercial companies, trade unions, associations and self-employed persons,_ *_within limits and for the needs of exercising their activities in a legal manne_*_r; (Art 94 (4))_
_-In the processes the subject_ *_thereof is the clients and customers_* _of institutions,_ *_commercial companies_*_, trade unions, associations and self-employed persons, within_ *_limits and for the needs of exercising their activities in a legal manner_*_; (Art 94 (5))_

*3. Missing list of examples:*  *Art. 98 of the Law provides that the MoET shall  make available to the public on its website, a list of possible processes that meet the licensing or authorisation requirements set out in Articles 95, 96, and 97 of the Law*. I was not able to find the list and *Dataguidance* (updated October 2022) mentioned that the list has yet to be published on the MoET website.

{color:#4c9aff}*Summary:* {color}*Provided that data "only" will be processed within the limits and for the requirements of the exercise of our activities in a lawful manner, there is no requirement of notifying the authorities. However, due to the lack of a comparison list, it is not possible to conclusively assess whether Selfie Verification falls within the limits and needs of exercising our business activities.*  


*Art 96 - Format and content requirements of the declaration*

*Add on:* Article 96 of the Law provides the format and content requirements of the declaration. Necessary information:
* objectives of the process;
* personal data, and the source thereof, under processing;
* categories of personal data concerned;
* third parties, or the categories thereof, who can view the data;  {color:#ff5630}_Authorities?_{color}
* data retention period;{color:#bf2600} _→ what is the retention time_{color}
* identity and address of the controller;
* identity and address of the controller in the event the said controller is residing outside the Lebanese territory;
* agency or agencies assigned with implementing the processing;{color:#ff5630}_→ What verification provider are we using?_ {color}
* person or agency exercising the right of access and how they exercise the said right;
* subcontractor, if any; {color:#ff5630}_→ What verification provider are we using?_ {color}
* method of access, or any other form of connection between data and other processes as well as possible data waivers to third parties where appropriate;
* actions taken to ensure the integrity of personal data and to ensure preservation of secrets protected under law, which are to be properly implemented by the controller; and
* emphasising that the processing shall be carried out in accordance with the law.


{color:#ff991f}+*Potential Fine:*+{color}
Anyone who has processed personal data without providing a permit or without obtaining a prior license before commencing its work in accordance with the Provisions of Section III of this Chapter should be penalized with a fine from one (1) million Lebanese Pounds to thirty (30) million Lebanese Pounds *(1857.18 Euro, 12.06.2023)*  and *imprisonment from three (3) months to three (3) years* _(Art 106)_

