Hi [~accountid:62bbfa39ec4c0d377f9fc3b7] , I added some minor comments/adjustments to the agreement. However, I believe the key issue here is to understand how the partnership will work in practice before actually signing the agreement. 

In Poland, [~accountid:6229fd926a4c4c0070b22ba6] insisted in signing a high level LOI, even though we have still not figured out the details of the partnership. So, we are now waiting to see how the negotiations of the truly important aspects of the partnership will go in order to prepare an agreement clearly setting out the obligations and roles of each party. Those include, for instance:

* Which data will or needs to be shared between the parties;
* Which Party will be responsible for what in terms of, for example, customer support;
* How to ensure a proper cooperation between the two Parties when it comes to management of claims and other angles of the partnership;
* Whether the partner will accept Bolt to review and amend its user Ts&Cs and Privacy Notice to make sure they are consistent with our requirements.

In my opinion, those practical details and others must be figured out before we sign an agreement with Aimo.

Another key point is that integrations such as this require a lot of effort from different teams within Bolt (Product, Tax, Commerce, Billing, Finance, besides Legal and Privacy ofc) and I’m not sure those teams are aware and ready to support this project. I talked to the Product Manager for Drive (Anton Diatlov) and the integration with Aimo is not in the Product’s team pipeline for Q3, which makes it very unlikely that other teams will have the resources to support the project in the next quarter. That’s a question to [~accountid:625fb4921046bb0071ddf448], but I’m sharing it with you because you may have some background I’m missing. Perhaps he’s looking further in the future.

Thanks and best,

Thiago

