* {{It should be noted that by this setup we do not have a good understanding of how our users' data is exactly going to be used (such as Ekata's product development and data analysis), so it could open ourselves up to possible reputational issues.}}

They would run the identity checks ( name, phone number, email, IP address) and give us the risk score. We discussed with [~accountid:712020:0b1ccfcb-3809-460a-88ab-c96985ba329a] that there is no need for Ekata to hold this data beyond the time it takes to run these checks.

* {{What touches upon the legal basis to share the rider's data with Ekata, we should ask for rider's consent, whereby we would need to expressly say what data is shared with whom and exactly for what reasons}}

We have done this on the driver side in order to share their data with MTN for the zero-rating project; we can make the privacy opt-in compulsory for new riders

* {{Nevertheless, as important is how we are going to be using this data received from Ekata internally - how do we exactly assess if the risk score is indicative of rider's safety and fraud risk actions? And will there be any actions taken on the basis of this data?}}

If a rider's risk score is high i.e due to their phone number or email address being new, the rider will be asked to go through a verification process to access the app i.e loading an photo of their ID document or a bank card. 

* {{You also need to consider that we should only store the received data that we really need and delete it as soon as possible.}}

I don’t see a reason for us to store the data either as the failed accounts will have to go through the same checks and be flagged as are high-risk if they try again.

